    model: 'Nausea, loss of appetite, yellow vision and a pulse of 52 are classic signs of digoxin toxicity. Furosemide is a loop diuretic that causes potassium loss, and low potassium makes the heart more sensitive to digoxin, so toxicity can occur even at a usual dose; older age and reduced kidney function also slow digoxin clearance. The team should hold the digoxin, notify the provider, obtain a serum digoxin level, potassium and magnesium, kidney function and an ECG, replace potassium as ordered and monitor the heart rhythm; digoxin immune fab is given for life-threatening toxicity. Prevention includes regular potassium and digoxin level checks, adjusting doses for kidney function, potassium supplements or a potassium-sparing strategy when appropriate, and teaching the patient to take his pulse and report nausea or vision changes.',
